Here's a rewritten version maintaining the original meaning and satirical spirit while using different phrasing:

Slick roads, folks catching colds and coughing,
Nobles wrapped in fur, poor souls suffering;
Wealthy titled men enjoying feasts,
While brilliant minds starve in attic rooms.

Grand estates, comfortable and vast;
Flatterers bowing yet grasping for more;
Rich misers ignoring the suffering poor;
Brave troops battling and bleeding.

Wives mocking their meek husbands;
Playhouses and worship halls;
Dance parties where foolish girls pine away;
Sickrooms filled with painful cries.

Culture and knowledge lamented;
Business declining, trust collapsing;
Officials scorning faithful citizens;
Royal divorces and marriages.

Writers unable to afford meals;
Clever cheats often prospering;
Runaways searching for refuge;
Hoarders stockpiling while merchants fail.

Skill and refinement abandoned entirely;
Honesty's principles twisted;
Arrogance rising above genuine worth;
True merit grieving in silence.

Women wagering away their hours;
Ignorant people mocking creative works;
Old women dressing as young girls,
While actual young women go unnoticed.

Some reveling in extravagance;
Others boasting more than battling;
Elderly suitors and feeble dandies;
Young lords hollow and dull.

Verse-makers, artists, and musicians;
Attorneys, physicians, statesmen:
Booklets, journals, and verses,
Chasing recognition through varied means.

Noble hearts with empty pockets;
Commanders better suited for sickbeds;
Young boys eager for battle,
Displacing experienced soldiers.

Decent men denied opportunities,
Scoundrels displaying shameless confidence;
Destruction accelerated, tranquility delayed;
Sincerity rejected, deception rewarded.
